<chapter xml:id="chemical-patterns-energy">
  <title>Chemical Patterns, Rates and Energy</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>This chapter extends the Chemical World through patterns in the Periodic Table, material properties, reaction rates and energy changes. The goal is to move from “what happened?” to models that explain and predict.</p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="cpe-atom-ratios">
    <title>Using the Periodic Table to predict atom ratios</title>
    <subsection><title>Read less</title>
      <p>Atoms combine in simple whole-number ratios. For common main-group elements, Periodic Table group patterns can help predict these ratios.</p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection><title>Standard</title>
      <p>Atoms form compounds in ratios that allow stable combinations of electrons. At Junior Cycle, simple group patterns can be used as a practical model. For example, Group 1 metals commonly form one positive ion while Group 17 non-metals commonly form one negative ion, so their compounds often have a 1:1 atom ratio.</p>
      <p>A Group 2 metal commonly forms a two-positive ion. Combining with a Group 17 element therefore usually requires two negative ions for every positive ion, giving a 1:2 atom ratio in the simplest formula.</p>
      <example><title>Magnesium chloride</title><p>Magnesium is treated as forming Mg²⁺ and chlorine as Cl⁻. Two chloride ions balance one magnesium ion, giving MgCl₂.</p></example>
    </subsection>
    <subsection><title>Go deeper</title>
      <p>The school “valency” pattern is a useful simplified model, not a universal rule for all elements and compounds. Transition metals and molecular compounds can require richer bonding models. The important scientific habit is to know the domain in which a shortcut is valid.</p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="cpe-material-properties">
    <title>Investigating material properties</title>
    <subsection><title>Read less</title>
      <p>Materials differ in solubility, electrical conductivity, melting point and boiling point. These properties help identify substances and choose materials for a purpose.</p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection><title>Standard</title>
      <p>A fair comparison keeps test conditions consistent. Conductivity can be compared with the same circuit and sample geometry. Solubility comparisons require the same solvent amount and temperature. Melting and boiling measurements require careful heating and suitable apparatus.</p>
      <p>Properties are evidence about structure. Metals often conduct electricity well because mobile charge carriers can move through the solid. Many molecular substances do not conduct because they lack mobile charged particles.</p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection><title>Go deeper</title>
      <p>“Soluble” is incomplete unless the solvent and conditions are stated. Solubility is a relationship between solute, solvent and conditions such as temperature, not a permanent label attached to one substance alone.</p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="cpe-reaction-rate">
    <title>Rate of reaction</title>
    <subsection><title>Read less</title>
      <p>Reaction rate tells how quickly reactants are used or products are formed. Rate can change with temperature, concentration, surface area and catalysts.</p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection><title>Standard</title>
      <p>A reaction requires reacting particles to encounter one another in suitable ways. Increasing temperature usually increases particle motion and the fraction of collisions energetic enough to react. Increasing concentration places more reacting particles in a given volume, increasing collision frequency. Increasing surface area exposes more particles at a solid surface.</p>
      <p>A catalyst increases reaction rate without being consumed overall. Catalysts are central to industry and biology; enzymes are biological catalysts.</p>
      <example><title>Measuring rate</title><p>For a gas-producing reaction, rate can be followed by measuring gas volume at regular time intervals or by measuring mass decrease if gas escapes safely from the apparatus.</p></example>
    </subsection>
    <subsection><title>Go deeper</title>
      <p>A factor can change rate without changing the final amount of product determined by the limiting reactant. Faster does not automatically mean “more product”. This distinction separates kinetics from reaction yield.</p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="cpe-gases">
    <title>Producing and identifying common gases</title>
    <subsection><title>Read less</title>
      <p>Chemical reactions can produce gases. Gas tests should be treated as evidence from a controlled observation, not as magic colour tricks.</p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection><title>Standard</title>
      <p>Hydrogen can be produced in some metal-acid reactions and is identified cautiously by its characteristic response to a lit splint. Oxygen supports combustion and can relight a glowing splint. Carbon dioxide turns suitable limewater cloudy because a precipitate forms.</p>
      <p>Gas collection method depends on properties such as solubility and density. Safety considerations determine scale, reactant choice and whether ignition tests are appropriate.</p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection><title>Go deeper</title>
      <p>A positive gas test supports identification but is strongest when combined with knowledge of the reaction and controls. Scientific identification uses converging evidence rather than a single memorised cue.</p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="cpe-biochemical-rate">
    <title>Rates in biochemical reactions</title>
    <subsection><title>Read less</title>
      <p>Enzyme-controlled reactions are affected by conditions such as temperature, pH and substrate concentration.</p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection><title>Standard</title>
      <p>Increasing temperature can increase reaction rate up to an optimum by increasing molecular motion. At sufficiently high temperatures, an enzyme's structure can change so activity falls sharply. pH can also alter interactions important for enzyme shape and function.</p>
      <p>A biochemical-rate investigation should measure an observable outcome over time while controlling other variables.</p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection><title>Go deeper</title>
      <p>An optimum is not a universal constant for “all enzymes”. Different enzymes evolved or operate in different environments and can have different temperature and pH profiles.</p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="cpe-exothermic-endothermic">
    <title>Exothermic and endothermic reactions</title>
    <subsection><title>Read less</title>
      <p>An exothermic process transfers energy from the reacting system to the surroundings. An endothermic process transfers energy into the reacting system from the surroundings.</p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection><title>Standard</title>
      <p>A temperature rise in well-designed conditions can provide evidence that energy has been transferred to the surroundings. A temperature fall can provide evidence of energy transfer from surroundings into the system.</p>
      <p>Combustion is commonly exothermic. Some thermal decompositions require continuing energy input and are treated as endothermic processes.</p>
      <p>Do not define exothermic simply as “gets hot”. The scientific definition concerns the direction of energy transfer between system and surroundings.</p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection><title>Go deeper</title>
      <p>Bond breaking requires energy and bond formation releases energy. The overall energy change depends on the balance between these processes, helping explain why reactions with similar-looking ingredients can have very different thermal effects.</p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="cpe-activation-energy">
    <title>Activation energy and energy profiles</title>
    <subsection><title>Read less</title>
      <p>Activation energy is the minimum energy barrier that reacting particles must overcome for a reaction pathway to proceed.</p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection><title>Standard</title>
      <p>An energy profile plots energy against reaction progress. Reactants begin at one energy level, the curve rises to a maximum representing the activation-energy barrier, and products finish at a different energy level.</p>
      <p>For an exothermic reaction, products are lower in energy than reactants. For an endothermic reaction, products are higher. A catalyst provides an alternative pathway with lower activation energy but does not change the overall energy difference between reactants and products.</p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection><title>Go deeper</title>
      <p>Activation energy explains why energetically favourable reactions may not occur rapidly at room temperature. Thermodynamic possibility and reaction rate are different questions.</p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="cpe-rate-investigation">
    <title>Design a reaction-rate investigation</title>
    <subsection><title>Read less</title>
      <p>Choose one rate factor to vary, measure a reaction outcome over time and keep other important conditions controlled.</p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection><title>Standard</title>
      <p>Possible designs include changing acid concentration while measuring gas production from a fixed mass and surface area of carbonate, or changing temperature while measuring the time for a visible change. Select a method that generates quantitative evidence rather than only “fast” and “slow” labels.</p>
      <p>Plot product amount against time or compare time to a defined endpoint. Repeat measurements and evaluate whether the chosen endpoint introduces judgement uncertainty.</p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection><title>Go deeper</title>
      <p>If measuring time to a fixed endpoint, shorter time indicates a greater average rate only because the same amount of observable change is being compared. The reciprocal idea is useful but should be justified rather than memorised.</p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
